          %p
            witnessing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            as the right Kan extension of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            along
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \circ \phi
            witnesses
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \circ f
            as the right Kan extension of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %p
            If the companions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            exist, such a cell is exact just when the canonical cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo ⊙
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \odot g_{*} \Rightarrow f_{*} \odot K
            is invertible. See Section 4 of
            %a{:href => "#Koudenburg2014"} Koudenburg 2014
            for more details.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#comma_squares Comma squares
          %p
            In ordinary category theory, any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+square"} comma square
            is always exact. In other words, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v/g)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            the canonical projections, then the square is exact. If additionally
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=*
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            simply picks out an object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=v/d
            is the comma category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_! F
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
            is the colimit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            pulled back to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v/d
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Lan
                    %mi v
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Lan_v F)(d) = colim_{v(b) \to d} F(b)
          %p which is (for ordinary categories) one of the equivalent definitions of when a Kan extension is pointwise.
          %p This example also makes clear why we define exactness only relative to pointwise Kan extensions. If we allowed non-pointwise ones, then we would have hardly any exact squares. Exactness is essentially a generalized version of pointwiseness, telling us how Kan extensions along one functor can be computed by transferring them to another functor, but for non-pointwise Kan extensions (which “exist by accident”) we have basically no hope of doing this.
          %h3#fully_faithful_functors Fully faithful functors
          %p
            A functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon A\to B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
            if and only if the square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi id
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi id
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi u
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{id}{\to} &amp; A\\
                  ^{id}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^u\\
                  A &amp; \underset{u}{\to} &amp; B}
          %p
            is exact. This just says that the unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A \to u^* u_!
            is an isomorphism, i.e. that left (and equivalently right) Kan extensions along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            are “honest” extensions.
          %h3#final_functors Final functors
          %p
            A functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon A\to B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
            if and only if the square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  *&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; *}
          %p
            is exact. Exactness of this square says that for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon B\to M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi B
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_A u^*F \to colim_B F
            is an isomorphism, which is one equivalent definition of when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is final.
          %h3#adjunctions Adjunctions
          %p
            A functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            is left adjoint to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u: B \to A
            with unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi uf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta: 1_A \implies uf
            if and only if the square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi id
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi η
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{id}{\to} &amp; A \\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; ^\eta\swArrow &amp; \downarrow^{id}\\
                  B&amp; \underset{u}{\to} &amp; A}
          %p
            is exact. Dually,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \dashv u
            with counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo :
                  %mi fu
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon: fu \implies 1_B
            if and only if the square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi id
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi ε
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi id
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{B &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; A\\
                  ^{id}\downarrow &amp; ^\varepsilon \swArrow &amp; \downarrow^f\\
                  B&amp; \underset{id}{\to} &amp; B}
          %p is exact.
          %h2#characterization Characterization
          %p Exact squares can be characterized in several ways, which generalize in different directions.
          %h3#using_profunctors Using profunctors
          %p
            Recall that for any functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon A\to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_!
            can be computed as the colimit weighted by the corepresentable profunctor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^*
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is the colimit weighted by the representable profunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,u)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H K
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_! f^*
            can be computed as a
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1) \circ B(1,f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^* v_!
            can be computed as a
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(1,g) \circ D(v,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(1,g) \circ D(v,1) \cong D(v,g)
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+reduction"} Yoneda reduction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1) \circ B(1,f) \to D(v,g)
          %p
            is an isomorphism. If the categories are large,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1)\circ B(1,f)
            may not exist
            = succeed "," do
              %em a priori
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1), B(1,f)\to D(v,g)
            realizes the composition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1)\circ B(1,f)
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(v,g)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to [B^{op},Set]
            it is easy to see that this condition is necessary as well. Unraveling it, we see that it says that for all objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int^{a\in A} C(u(a),c) \times B(b,f(a)) \to D(v(b), g(c))
          %p is an isomorphism.
          %p
            So far, this argument works to describe the exact squares in arbitrary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+equipped+with+proarrows"} 2-category equipped with proarrows
            where we have a sensible notion of profunctor that describes to the notion of pointwise Kan extension under consideration. However, in the Set-based case, we can go further. Given the construction of coequalizers in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi g
                  %mi u
                  %mo →
                  %mi v
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \colon g u \to v f
            the given 2-cell, we have
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                For any morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\colon v(b) \to g(c)
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
                and morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\colon u(a) \to c
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta\colon b\to f(a)
                such that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi φ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\alpha) \circ \psi_a \circ v(\beta) = \varphi
            %li
              %p
                For any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo ,
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,\alpha,\beta)
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi β
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a',\alpha',\beta')
                as above with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ′
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi β
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\alpha) \circ \psi_a \circ v(\beta) = g(\alpha') \circ \psi_{a'} \circ v(\beta')
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag"} zigzag
                of arrows connecting
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
                and rendering the evident induced diagram commutative.
          %p
            We can state this equivalently as follows. Given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b/A/c)
            to be the category whose objects are triples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,\alpha,\beta)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\colon u(a) \to c
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta\colon b\to f(a)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to a'
            making two triangles commute. There is a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b/A/c) \to D(v(b),g(c))
          %p
            (considering the latter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homset"} homset
            as a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,\alpha,\beta)
            to the composite
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\alpha) \circ \psi_a \circ v(\beta)
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
          %h4#examples_2 Examples
          .un_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p Note that in the case of a square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi u
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; B\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                    *&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; *}
            %p
              this characterization reduces to saying that for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b/u
              is connected. This is the standard combinatorial characterization of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
          .un_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              This characterization implies directly that any
              %span.newWikiWord
                cocomma square
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cocomma+square"} ?
              is exact, since the cocomma object of a cospan
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi u
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \overset{u}{\leftarrow} A \overset{f}{\to} B
              is precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cograph+of+a+profunctor"} cograph
              of the profunctor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(u,1) \circ B(1,f)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+exact+squares"} homotopy exact squares
              unless we take a “homotopy cocomma object”).
          %h3#CommaObj Using comma objects
          %p Another approach is to argue as follows. First note that any comma square of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi d
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi v
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi d
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{v/d &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; C\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow^v\\
                  * &amp; \underset{d}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p must be exact. As observed above, this is one of the equivalent definitions of what it means to be a pointwise left Kan extension. (Note that this ejects us from the world of enriched categories already.) Dually,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi d
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{d/g &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; *\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow &amp; \downarrow^d\\
                  B &amp; \underset{g}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p must also be exact, by definition of pointwise right Kan extensions. Now suppose that a square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi v
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^u\downarrow &amp; \swArrow &amp; \downarrow^v\\
                  C&amp; \underset{g}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p
            is given. The Beck-Chevalley transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_! f^* \to g^* v_!
            between functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to M
            will be an isomorphism as soon as it is an isomorphism componentwise, i.e. when evaluated at every object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^* u_! f^* \to c^* g^* v_!
            to be an isomorphism. Now consider the composite square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi c
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi q
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi v
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi c
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{u/c &amp; \overset{q}{\to} &amp; A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^p\downarrow &amp; \swArrow &amp; \downarrow^u &amp; \swArrow &amp; \downarrow^v\\
                  * &amp; \underset{c}{\to}&amp; C &amp; \underset{g}{\to} &amp; D.}
          %p
            Since the left-hand square is of the form considered above, it is exact, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_! q^* \to c^* u_!
            is an isomorphism. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^* u_! f^* \to c^* g^* v_!
            is an isomorphism if and only if the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_! q^* f^* \to c^* u_! f^* \to c^* g^* v_!
          %p is an isomorphism, but this is just the Beck-Chevalley transformation for the composite square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi c
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi v
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{u/c &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow &amp; \downarrow^v\\
                  * &amp; \underset{g(c)}{\to} &amp; D.}
          %p
            So the given square is exact just when all of these squares are exact. But we can also play the same game dually, so the given square is exact just when for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mi v
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{(b/A/c) &amp; \overset{x}{\to} &amp; *\\
                  ^y\downarrow &amp;\swArrow &amp; \downarrow^{v(b)}\\
                  * &amp; \underset{g(c)}{\to} &amp; D.}
          %p is exact. However, the comma square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi z
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mi v
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{D(v(b),g(c)) &amp; \overset{z}{\to} &amp; *\\
                  ^w\downarrow &amp;\swArrow &amp; \downarrow^{v(b)}\\
                  * &amp; \underset{g(c)}{\to} &amp; D.}
          %p
            is always exact, as observed previously, and by the universal property of a comma square, the previous one factors through this one via a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\colon (b/A/c) \to D(v(b),g(c))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi w
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = w r
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = z r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b/A/c)
            square factors as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo *
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mo !
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi r
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≅
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_! x^* \cong w_! r_! r^* z^* \to w_! z^* \overset{\cong}{\to} g(c)^* v(b)_!.
          %p
            Hence it is an isomorphism if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_! x^* \to w_! z^*
            is an isomorphism, which is to say that colimits of constant diagrams on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b/A/c)
            and on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(v(b),g(c))
            agree. But the colimit of a constant diagram is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} copower
            with the set of connected components of the domain category, so we recover the same characterization as before.
          %p
            While this argument does not generalize to general enriched categories and proarrow equipments, it does generalize in a different direction. The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
            is essentially designed exactly so that this argument works, up until the last step: in a general derivator, colimits of constant diagrams may depend on more (or less) than the set of connected components of the domain. For instance, in the derivator of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of the domain category, a finer invariant than its
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+exact+squares"} homotopy exact squares
            for computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extensions"} homotopy Kan extensions
            in derivators: the squares where the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b/A/c) \to D(v(b),g(c))
            induces a weak equivalence of nerves.
          %h2#references References
          %p Some of the early theory is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ren%C3%A9+Guitart"}> René Guitart
              ,
              %em Relations et carrés exacts,
              Ann. Sc. Math. Qué., juillet 1980, vol. IV, N° 2, p. 103-125.
          %p For double categories, see Section 4 of
          %ul
            %li#Koudenburg2014
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seerp+Roald+Koudenburg"}> Seerp Roald Koudenburg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On pointwise Kan extensions in double categories
